+# `@frontend-architect-eval/feature-flags`
+
+A Vite plugin that exposes an explicit, typed subset of a server-side JSON
+configuration through `virtual:feature-flags`. It supports Vite development,
+production builds, build watch mode, and HMR without rewriting unrelated source
+text.
+
+## Usage
+
+```ts
+// vite.config.ts
+import { featureFlags } from '@frontend-architect-eval/feature-flags'
+import { defineConfig } from 'vite'
+
+export default defineConfig({
+  plugins: [
+    featureFlags({
+      source: new URL('./flags.json', import.meta.url),
+      expose: ['checkoutV2', 'theme'],
+    }),
+  ],
+})
+```
+
+Register the virtual module declaration once in the consumer's `tsconfig.json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "compilerOptions": {
+    "types": ["@frontend-architect-eval/feature-flags/virtual"]
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Then import the immutable client snapshot:
+
+```ts
+import { flags } from 'virtual:feature-flags'
+
+if (flags.checkoutV2) {
+  // render the public experiment
+}
+```
+
+Without an application declaration, flag values use the safe
+`boolean | number | string` union. Applications can declare exact keys and
+narrow their values with declaration merging:
+
+```ts
+// feature-flags.d.ts (included by the application's tsconfig)
+import '@frontend-architect-eval/feature-flags'
+
+declare module '@frontend-architect-eval/feature-flags' {
+  interface FeatureFlagDefinitions {
+    readonly checkoutV2: boolean
+    readonly theme: 'light' | 'dark'
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Once merged, misspelled or undeclared keys are TypeScript errors. Every declared
+value must still be assignable to `boolean | number | string`; otherwise the
+virtual module resolves to `never` and the consumer type-check fails.
+
+`FeatureFlagsOptions`, `FeatureFlagValue`, `FeatureFlagDefinitions`,
+`FeatureFlagsErrorCode`, and `FeatureFlagsConfigError` are public exports. The
+package is ESM-only and declares Vite as a peer because the host owns the Vite
+instance.
+
+## Data and security contract
+
+`source` is a UTF-8 JSON object. A relative path is resolved against Vite's
+project root; a URL must use `file:`. Every exposed value must be a string,
+finite number, or boolean.
+
+`expose` is mandatory and is the only data allowed to cross into browser code.
+The plugin never serializes the complete JSON object, never lists unrelated keys
+in diagnostics, and never places the original JSON or its path into a client
+source map. Treat every key named in `expose` as public: no plugin can infer
+whether an intentionally allowlisted value is actually a credential. Keep
+tokens and other server-only configuration out of this list and enforce
+authorization on the server rather than with these flags.
+
+Configuration failures use stable `FeatureFlagsConfigError.code` values and
+identify the source, option or public key plus the corrective action. Validation
+runs when Vite resolves configuration, so a bad source fails startup/build even
+when no module imports the flags. Invalid edits during HMR use Vite's normal
+error path instead of silently serving stale configuration.
+
+## Lifecycle, cache, and source maps
+
+Each plugin instance owns one generated-module cache. Its identity consists of
+that instance's resolved source path and `expose` list. Vite configuration fixes
+those inputs; a source-file watch event clears the cache, and HMR validates the
+new file before invalidating `virtual:feature-flags`. Build watch and dev use the
+same loader as a one-shot production build.
+
+The virtual module returns a source map whose source identity is
+`virtual:feature-flags`. `sourcesContent` contains only the generated,
+allowlisted client module; it never embeds the server JSON. Downstream Vite
+source-map settings therefore cannot reintroduce filtered fields.
+
+## Release verification
+
+From the workspace root, run:
+
+```sh
+npm run check
+```
+
+The checks build declarations and maps, type-check a real consumer, build that
+consumer with source maps, exercise a Vite dev server plus a source-file HMR
+change, assert actionable invalid-config diagnostics, and install the packed
+tarball into a clean project. The artifact check is important because source
+tests cannot detect missing declaration or export files.
+
+Before publishing, test every Vite major claimed by `peerDependencies`; add a
+matrix entry before widening that range. Inspect both generated JavaScript and
+`.map` files for a known unexposed sentinel whenever serialization changes.
+
+## Version evolution
+
+Use SemVer from the consumer's perspective:
+
+- adding optional plugin options, error codes, or newly exported types is
+  normally minor;
+- changing default value types, virtual-module exports, validation timing,
+  path resolution, HMR semantics, or the meaning of `expose` is breaking;
+- fixing leakage or accepting another valid input without changing existing
+  behavior is normally patch-level.
+
+For a breaking contract, ship a prerelease, document the old and new config,
+and keep a deprecated compatibility path for at least one planned migration
+window when it can preserve the security invariant. Never provide a legacy
+fallback that exposes the whole source file. A future alternative source format
+belongs behind the internal load/validate boundary; the virtual module and
+allowlist contract should remain stable.
